---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { cases } from "../../data/profile";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const item = cases.find((caseItem) => caseItem.slug === "apit")!;
---
<BaseLayout title="APIT" description="APIT AI Capstone project case study." >
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-kicker">
        <span class="tag">{item.label}</span>
        <span class="tag">Role: {item.role}</span>
        <span class="tag">3-member team</span>
      </div>
      <h1>APIT: LLM-powered API test generation as an AI capstone.</h1>
      <p class="page-summary">{item.summary}</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href={`${base}docs/apit-capstone.pdf`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open capstone PDF</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}projects/`}>Back to projects</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tight">
    <div class="container content-grid">
      <article class="article">
        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Context</div>
          <h2>The capstone targets the Testing & Integration phase of SDLC.</h2>
          <p>APIT is positioned as an intelligent QA enhancement: it reads standardized API documentation, defines test scope, generates structured test cases, executes them, and reports pass/fail/exception outcomes through a dashboard.</p>
          <p>The gap is clear: execution tools such as Postman, JMeter, RestAssured, PyTest, and Newman are useful, but they still depend heavily on manually designed test cases and do not solve intelligent generation by themselves.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">My role</div>
          <h2>System Architecture Designer.</h2>
          <p>The project document records my role as the system architecture designer, responsible for designing the required system and optimizing system performance. In portfolio terms, this should be framed as system design, workflow design, and QA-domain contribution rather than claiming every model/data task individually.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Pipeline</div>
          <h2>From API docs to evaluated test cases.</h2>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Document preprocessing</b><span>Standardize API docs from OpenAPI, Swagger, PDF, or structured text.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Test scope</b><span>Select requirements, endpoints, and value/risk boundaries for generation.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>LLM generation</b><span>Fine-tuned model + RAG generates structured test cases and reasoning.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Execution/report</b><span>Run requests, classify pass/fail/exception, and present results.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Data and model</div>
          <h2>Controlled synthetic data and small-model adaptation.</h2>
          <p>The dataset strategy is one of the strongest parts of the project: manually written realistic API documents were distilled into prompts, used to generate controlled API documentation, and then converted into desired test-case outputs with reasoning.</p>
          <p>The resulting dataset contained 1,258 samples with an approximately 8:1:1 train/validation/test split and bilingual English/Vietnamese coverage. Qwen-2.5-3B and Llama-3.2-3B were fine-tuned with LoRA/4-bit training under identical hyperparameters.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="block">
          <div class="eyebrow">Evaluation</div>
          <h2>Structured output quality was measured, not guessed.</h2>
          <p>APIT evaluated generated test cases with reference-based precision, recall, and F1. Matching used fuzzy string similarity, numeric tolerance, exact matching for categorical fields, and handling for malformed JSON outputs.</p>
          <p>The project also evaluated reasoning quality with logical validity, coherence, and groundedness, which maps well to the Agentic QA direction.</p>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="sidebar">
        <div class="card side-card">
          <h3>Result</h3>
          <p>Qwen-2.5-3B LoRA achieved 0.655 macro-F1 on structured API test generation, outperforming the Llama-3.2-3B LoRA baseline under the same setup.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card side-card">
          <h3>Stack</h3>
          <div class="tag-row" style="padding-top:0">
            {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card side-card">
          <h3>Spec</h3>
          <dl class="spec-list">
            <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>{item.role}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Dataset</dt><dd>1,258 samples</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Models</dt><dd>Qwen-2.5-3B, Llama-3.2-3B</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Method</dt><dd>LoRA / 4-bit training</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>